      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. So the stability depends on that competition between the H+ ions and the metals for those binding sites.\n\nI'm trying to wrap my head around the \"disappearance\" aspect though. Even after accounting for the chelation, there still feels like this level of \"clandestine\" retention in the subsurface that's hard to quantify with just the $K_f$ values. It's like there's an extra layer of unavailability happening in the dark, recessed areas of the soil profile—almost as if the minerals are being intentionally stashed in these hidden, unreachable niches.\n\nIs there any additional physical or chemical factor that would explain why these nutrients seem to \"vanish\" so effectively once they reach those deeper, more complex spaces in the soil matrix? I'm looking for anything related to micro-scale sequestration or the way they might be effectively \"looted\" from the available pool by the soil structure itself.",
